5. The appellant, as plaintiffs, instituted Title Suit being No. 958 of 2008 in the Court of Joint District Judge, 7th Court, Dhaka for partition along with a prayer for declaration that the deed being No. 6391 dated 11-10-1968 is forged, fraudulent, ineffective and not binding upon the plaintiff. The case of the plaintiffs in short is that the original owner and possessor of the suit land was Sekina Begum, maternal grand mother of the plaintiff petitioner and respondent Nos. 2 and 3 and the mother of respondent No.1, who purchased the said land vide registered deed No. 3427 dated 4-7-1947 from one Shaimaty Shantipoda Debi. Shakina Begum was owning and possessing the said land including pond and building with an area of 0.65 acres of land. She died leaving behind plaintiff and defendant No. 1 and also defendant Nos. 2 and 3 as her legal heirs. The said property was not partitioned amongst the said heirs, and now the heirs of the late Shakina Begum have been owning and possessing the said land as ejmali property. The defendant No. 1 is the son of Sakina Bibi and defendant No. 3 is the son of Begum Sayeda. Begum Sayeda died leaving behind one son Md Farook, the plaintiff petitioner and defendant Nos. 2 and 3 and the suit properties of Begum Sayeda i.e. all the heirs have been possessing the said land at their sweet will and the plaintiff-petitioner has been possessing a small part of the suit land and the rest of the land has been in possession of the defendant respondent opposite parties which are more land than that of the plaintiff-petitioner land, the plaintiff-petitioner is the grandson (Nati) of late Shakina Begum who will get 2/12 shares amounting to 1083 ajutangsha, and on the other hand defendant Nos. 2 and 3 would get 1083 ajutangsha, the plaintiff petitioner asked the defendant-opposite party No.1 Serajul Islam to give their share but on reply he stated that they will not get any share of the suit land as the mother of the defendant No. 1 Shakina Begum gifted all the property of the suit land in favour of defendant opposite party No. 1 and after hearing this the plaintiff-petitioner on 21-1-2008 found out that he created a deed by false personification which was clearly described in the schedule of kha and the said deed was created and the mother of the defendant-opposite party No. 1 was not owning of the said land as the owner and possessor of the said land was Abdul Matin Dhali. The husband of Shakina Begum and their heirs was alive while the deed was created by the defendant-respondent opposite party No.1 which are false, fabricated and created by false personification and while the said deed was created by false personification and while the said deed was created the defendant-opposite party No. 1 was in France. So the possession of the said property was not at all handed over to him which was the main ingredient of a deed of heba-bil-ewaz which was not at all complied with. Moreso, the owner and possessor of the property mentioned in the schedule Mosammat Shakina Begum purchased the said by a registered deed being No. 3427 dated 4-7-1949 and the suit land was an ejmali property which was never partitioned between the heirs of Mosammat Shakina Begum by mates and bounds as a result the plaintiff-petitioner is unable to develop the said property. The cause of action firstly arose on 10-1-2008 when the defendant denied to partition the said property for which the plaintiff-petitioner was compelled to file the suit for partition with declaration that the deed in question is not binding upon the plaintiff as it is false and concocted to the said deed. 

 9. Mr AK Rashedul Huq, the learned Advocate appearing on behalf of the respondents opposite parties vehemently opposes the appeal as well as Rules. He submits that the, Court below on proper appreciation of the facts and circumstances and the provision of law has rightly rejected the plaint and thereby the same requires no interference by this Court. The learned Advocate referred the plaint as well as the written statement and submits that while rejecting the plaint the Court below came to a conclusion regarding the question of limitation in a lawful manner as well as addressed the other issues which are very much legal and acceptable as well as applicable for 'invoking the provision laid down in Order VII, rule 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure. He submits that apart from the provision as laid down in Order VII, rule 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure a Court of law exercising the power conferred under section 151 of the Code can also reject a plaint: if the same is not covered within the ground as stipulated in Order VII, rule 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure. He submits that exercising the inherent power the Court of law reserved the absolute power to reject a plaint if it is apparent that the further proceeding of a suit is fruitless. The learned Advocate placed certain facts and circumstances and submits that there are serious suppression 'of fad and declaration as sought relates to. the deed which is a very old one as well as the old description to the suit property was not there though the plaintiffs is praying for decree of partition at well as there are certain admissions by the plaintiff in the plaint regarding the Heba-bil-ewaz. He submits that the present case in hand not only is hit by the provision 'Order VII, rule 11(D) of the Code but also attract the provision of section 151 of the Code as it is apparent on the face of the record of the instant suit is nothing but a fruitless litigation and harassing the parties for indefinite period. He Submits that the plaintiff failed to proof their claim in the plaint in an appropriate manner rather the plaint was not drawn in accordance with the, provision of the Code of Civil Procedure as the grounds as pressed and stated by the Court below while rejecting the plaint are fit and proper ground as such, the impugned judgment and decree is liable to be sustained for ends of justice. He submits that it is apparent from the plaint that there is no cause of action rather the plaintiffs failed to make out any prima-facie case regarding the question of limitation, cause Of action as well as other issues and, as such, the trial Court committed no illegality in rejecting the, plaint. The learned Advocate also submits that apart from invocation of section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure, the Court of law while rejecting the plaint can travel beyond the plaint to ascertain documents and materials which are admitted in the plaint itself and, as such, there is no harm in relying upon the documents and materials on record beyond the plaint which are admitted by the parties in the plaint and in the present case in hand the other materials which includes written statement filed by the defendants are admitted by both the parties and, as such, the Court below committed no illegality in rejecting the plaint summarily. Regarding injunction he submits that since the plaint was rejected as such, the appellant plaintiffs are not entitled to get any injunction as much as there was no violation as because the said show cause was in existence much earlier before filing of the suit in hand. In support of his contention the learned Advocate also relied upon the decision reported 50 DLR (AD) 99. He also referred the decisions reported in 35 DLR 39, PLR (2008) (SC) 371, 51 DLR (AD) 155, 49 DLR (AD) 151, 54 DLR 310, 22 BLD (HCD) 423, 2002 (SCR) 338 and 53 DLR (AD) 12. 

 12. Admittedly the suit is for partition as well as declaration to the effect that a deed is not binding upon the plaintiffs as much as fraudulent and collusive one. It further appears from the lower Court record that the defendants before filing of the application under Order VII, rule 11 of the Code filed detailed written statement denying all the material allegation made by the plaintiffs as, much as, in the written statement the defendant, narrated their case in a detailed manner. Order VII, rule 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure deals with the provision relates to the rejection of plaint which runs as follows; 

