 21. By the aforesaid findings the Court below came to a conclusion that the claim of the plaintiff are not sustainable and there is no cause 'of action as much as the plaintiffs failed to prove their prima-facie right and title. Admittedly in the decisions as referred by the learned Advocate for the appellant this Court as well as our Apex Court came to a conclusion that the question of limitation can only be considered if there is no factual aspect available. As per their Lordships when the question of limitation about such facts that should be disposed of by taking evidence after framing issues. Also in the said decisions the High Court Division came to a conclusion that the Court should look into the averments made in the plaint and cannot go beyond same to find whether the plaint is liable to be rejected or not. As much as while considering the application for rejection of plaint there is no scope to consider the possible defence plea: Apart from that the Court further came to a conclusion that the plaint 'could be rejected 'only when the Court comes to the conclusion that even if all the averments made in the plaint are taken to be proved still that the plaintiffs could not be entitled to any relief at all. Also this Court as well as of our apex Court came to a conclusion that the question of limitation and the question of resjudicata and Others issues as raised in the application for rejection of the plaint are mixed questions of fact and law which needs through investigation on the basis of credible evidence for arriving in a correct decision by framing specific issues. In the case reported in 35 DLR 39 the Court below' deals with, the question of limitation but the same does not relate to rejection of plaint rather While deciding the judgment and order the Court came to a conclusion regarding the question of limitation in that case. In the case reported in PLD (2008) (SC) 31 the Court deals with the issue relating to the cause of action and came to a conclusion that it would be pertinent to mention here that the term cause of action represents all the requisites and the facts which are necessary for the plaintiff to prove, before he can succeed in a suit. In the case reported in 53 DLR (AD) 12 their Lordships came to a conclusion that if the ultimate result of the suit is as clear as day light such a suit should be buried at its inception So that no further time is consumed in a fruitless litigation. In the case reported in 51 DLR (AD) 155 their Lordships came to a Conclusion that in a partition suit in the hotchpotch the property are to be attracted in an appropriate manner. In the case reported in 49 DLR (AD) 151 their Lordships came to a conclusion that all the properties should be included in a suit for partition. A partial partition can be allowed, where it is not proved that the parties Will be prejudiced by such partition. In the case reported in 54 DLR 310, the Court came to a conclusion that it is not the relief which is claimed, rather it is the relief which can be given to the plaintiff, that has to be looked into and considered in a plaint. In the Case reported in 22 BLD 423 = 54 DLR 310 the Court came to a conclusion, where certain facts are brought to the notice of the Court, which is not mentioned in the plaint or which arises, subsequent to filing of the suit and accepted as true by both the parties, such a fact can be taken into consideration in considering rejecting of the plaint under Order VII, rule 11 of the Code. In a case reported in 2002 SCMR 338 (Supreme Court Of Pakistan) the Court came to a conclusion besides the averments made in the plaint, other material available on record which on its own strength was legally sufficient can be looked into for the purpose of rejection, of the plaint.
